1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 Hey everyone, this is Dr. Marcon and this is chapter 3 on cell structure and function.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 So here are some of the learning objectives for you for this chapter.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
 We want to relate the structure of a cell to its function.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 But first we have to figure out what is a cell and what's inside a cell.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 So eventually we'll be able to explain and identify how stuff, such as solutes and water,

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
 crosses membranes, determine the direction of the movement of water in different situations.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
 We'll talk about that when we talk about membrane transport.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 And then describe the key eukaryotic organelles and their functions,

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
 including those involved in building proteins.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 So, so far in the last couple chapters we've learned about the properties of life

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 and the basic building blocks, the basic elements that make us who we are.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 We talked about atoms, we talked about elements.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 So we went from the basic building blocks and now we're slowly building on

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 to the components that make us humans, that make us who we are.

15
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 So in the late 1830s we had two botanists.

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 We had a botanist as well, we had botanist, Matthias, Schleiden and Zual,

17
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 just Theodore Schwan, that were studying tissues

18
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 and they proposed what's known as the unified cell theory.

19
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 So we talked about, recall that living things are made up of one or more cells.

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 So these two scientists actually made three postulates to the unified cell theory first.

21
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
 As we just said, all living things are composed of one or more cells.

22
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
 Also, the second, the cell is the basic unit of life.

23
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 So it is the basic building blocks of all living things.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
 And the third, all new cells will rise from existing cells.

25
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:06,000
 They just don't spontaneously come into existence, they come from existing cells.

26
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 And again, this is referred to as the cell theory or the unified cell theory.

27
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 So we have two main cell types.

28
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 We have prokaryotic cells and we have eukaryotic cells.

29
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 Prokaryotic cells, they lack an internal membrane-bound organelles.

30
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
 They are a lot smaller compared to eukaryotic cells.

31
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 Eukaryotic cells, we know as our animal cells, basically the cells that make us who we are.

32
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 They do contain an internal membrane-bound organelles, they are larger.

33
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 There are four common components between these two cells.

34
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 Depending on what book you read, there may be three, there may be four.

35
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:58,000
 According to your book, there are four common components between prokaryotic cells and eukaryotic cells.

36
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 First, a plasma membrane which is an outer covering that separates the cell's interior from its surrounding environment.

37
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 Second, an internal cytoplasm, this is the inside jelly-like region within the cell.

38
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
 Third, DNA.

39
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 DNA is the genetic material of the cell.

40
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
 And according to your book, there is a fourth common component which is ribosomes.

41
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
 Ribosomes are particles that synthesize proteins.

42
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:35,000
 And we will talk more about that as we talk about the eukaryotic cells.

43
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:41,000
 So depending on your book again, there may be three, there may be four.

44
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 But according to your book, there are four common components between these two types of cells.

45
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
 So how many cells are there in the human body?

46
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:03,000
 If you think about it, probably a lot more than 10 million but maybe less than a quadrillion.

47
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
 So there are 100 trillion cells in the human body and how big are these cells?

48
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:23,000
 So if you look at this table here, we can see that our cells are anywhere between 1 micron to 100 microns big.

49
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 So most plant and animal cells are between or within this range.

50
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 And we can see these cells are using different types of microscope.

51
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
 So cells are pretty tiny but how big is a micron?

52
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:50,000
 So if you think about it, when we look at a ruler, we have the metric systems that are using meters and centimeters.

53
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 We have one centimeter here.

54
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 So how many cells can fit in one centimeter?

55
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 So about 10,000 microns can fit in one centimeter.

56
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:12,000
 So each cell is about 10 microns big so about 1,000 cells can fit in one centimeter.

57
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
 So just a really quick overview.

58
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:22,000
 There are several very important scientists that actually made discoveries about cells.

59
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
 First we have Robert Hook.

60
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
 Robert Hook first observed plant cells with a very crude microscope in the late 1600s.

61
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
 Then we have Matthias Schladen and Theodore Schwan.

62
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:42,000
 Schladen and Schwan actually in the 1830s boldly asserted that all living things are composed of cells.

63
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:52,000
 Shortly after that we have Rudolf Vercho who extended this idea by contending that cells only come from other cells.

64
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:59,000
 Now this kind of challenged the prevailing theory of the time where people believe that organisms can arise from

65
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
 non-living matter and this was known as the theory of spontaneous generation.

66
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
 Basically living things just spontaneously generated.

67
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 But we know that that is not true.

68
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:16,000
 And Vercho's theory that cells coming from other cells actually still holds true today.

69
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:23,000
 So we know that cells are the small living units in the body.

70
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
 We know that cells can obtain nutrients, make molecules needed to survive,

71
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 dispose of waste, maintain the shape or their shape of the cell as well as replicate and make more cells.

72
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:43,000
 We have structures within the cells called organelles.

73
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
 Organelles are subunits of cells that have specific functions.

74
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
 And most cells do contain the same basic organelles.

75
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,000
 However, not all cells have all organelles in the same abundance.

76
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,000
 For example, we will talk about mitochondria.

77
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
 Mitochondria is an organelle that is responsible for ATP production.

78
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
 ATP is needed for energy.

79
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 And we have skeletal muscles that move and need ATP for energy.

80
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:19,000
 So cells within skeletal muscle will have more mitochondria so that they can have more ATP for movement.

81
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:28,000
 Whereas there are other cells who don't really use a lot of ATP, so they might not have as much mitochondria

82
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
 as say a skeletal muscle cell.

83
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
 Now, cells have three very main components.

84
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
 There is the outer boundary known as the plasma membrane.

85
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,000
 And then within the cell we have the cytoplasm, which contains most organelles.

86
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:55,000
 And the control center of the cell we know is the nucleus and the nucleus controls all cellular activities.

87
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,000
 And here is just a basic structure of a generalized cell.

88
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:09,000
 So we can see within the center of the cell is the nucleus contained within a nuclear envelope.

89
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:15,000
 We can see that there is a smaller structure called the nucleolus.

90
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
 And then we can see chromatin within the nucleus.

91
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:29,000
 And then we have this outer plasma membrane which basically holds the shape of the cell and kind of holds everything inside.

92
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:37,000
 And then within the cell we have the cytoplasm where we can find the different organelles within the cell.

93
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
 And then we'll go over the different organelles in just a little bit.

94
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
 So the plasma membrane defines the extent of the cell.

95
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:54,000
 It separates the intracellular fluid within the cell from the extra cellular fluid outside and between the cells.

96
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:06,000
 The structure of the plasma membrane, we know that it is based on a fluid mosaic model, meaning it has a lipid bilayer

97
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
 within the lipid bilayer.

98
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,000
 We can see types of membrane proteins throughout.

99
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
 For example, we have integral proteins.

100
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:23,000
 These integral proteins are firmly embedded in the plasma membrane or attached to the lipid bilayer.

101
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:31,000
 We also have short chains of carbohydrates that are attached to the integral proteins that form the glycocalyx.

102
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:36,000
 We also have peripheral proteins that attach to the membrane surface.

103
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:42,000
 These proteins support the plasma membrane from the cytoplasmic side.

104
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:49,000
 And here we can see the fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane.

105
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:58,000
 We see that lipid bilayer, so there are two layers of lipids forming the plasma membrane.

106
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:07,000
 We have an inward facing layer of the phospholipid and then the outward facing layer of the phospholipids.

107
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:15,000
 So we can see the head of the phospholipid and then the tails within.

108
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:24,000
 So two layers of lipids forming that lipid bilayer of the plasma membrane.

109
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:33,000
 So the phospholipid molecule is actually what makes up the bilayer of the plasma membrane.

110
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:38,000
 It has a polar head, which we can see by these little circles.

111
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:43,000
 And these polar parts of the phospholipid, they're actually hydrophilic.

112
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:47,000
 So, "philic" means something that is attracted to or like.

113
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:55,000
 So a hydrophilic end of the phospholipid molecule means that it actually is attracted to water.

114
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:01,000
 And then we have a non-polar tail of the phospholipid molecule.

115
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:06,000
 And these are actually hydrophobic, meaning they are not attracted to water.

116
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:11,000
 They're actually not afraid, but basically not attracted to.

117
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:15,000
 So we have two parts of the phospholipid molecule.

118
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:22,000
 So we have that polar hydrophilic head and then the non-polar tail, which is hydrophobic.

119
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
 And then in this picture, we can see the different proteins that we talked about.

120
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:32,000
 We have these integral proteins that are embedded throughout the phospholipid bilayer.

121
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 We also have peripheral proteins located on the surfaces of the phospholipid bilayer.

122
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:53,000
 And then we have the carbohydrates that are attached to or can be attached to the plasma membrane forming a glyco-calix.

123
00:11:53,000 --> 00:12:07,000
 So functions of the plasma membrane, basically, they relate to the location at the interface of the cell's exterior or outer part and the interior, which is the inner part.

124
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:11,000
 The plasma membrane can provide a barrier against substances outside the cell.

125
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:22,000
 And then we do have some plasma membrane that do act as receptors for certain things, such as hormones or neurotransmitters.

126
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:29,000
 Other functions of the plasma membrane can also include determining what substances can enter or leave the cell.

127
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:44,000
 So we know that the membrane is selectively permeable, meaning it can basically decide what can come into the cell or what can leave the cell.

128
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:54,000
 Plasma membrane is important for transport of molecules within the cell or leaving the cell.

129
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:01,000
 So type of membrane transport we're going to first talk about is the first one will be simple diffusion.

130
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:12,000
 So simple diffusion is basically the tendency of molecules to move down their concentration gradient from an area of high concentration to an area below concentration.

131
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
 We then have osmosis.

132
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
 Whenever you hear the word osmosis, think water.

133
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:22,000
 So osmosis is basically the diffusion of water molecules across a membrane.

134
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,000
 Note that simple diffusion and osmosis do not require energy.

135
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:34,000
 So it's basically a simple diffusion of areas of high concentration to low.

136
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:43,000
 In the case of osmosis, that being the movement of water molecules.

137
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:48,000
 So here we can see in this first part of the diagram, simple diffusion.

138
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:56,000
 We have simple diffusion of fat soluble molecules that go directly through that phospholipid bilayer down their concentration gradient.

139
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:01,000
 So going from an area of high concentration to an area of low concentration.

140
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:06,000
 Osmosis, this is the diffusion of water through the lipid bilayer.

141
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:14,000
 Again, these two types of membrane transport do not require ATP or do not require energy.

142
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:19,000
 This next part of the diagram we'll see, we can see facilitated diffusion.

143
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:28,000
 Now facilitated diffusion is a type of membrane transport where we have movement of molecules down their concentration gradient.

144
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
 However, using an integral protein.

145
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:36,000
 So we talked about integral proteins being embedded within that phospholipid bilayer.

146
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:48,000
 Facilitated diffusion is basically the diffusion of molecules going from an area of high concentration to an area of low concentration with the help of an integral protein.

147
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:54,000
 So facilitated basically means it requires help.

148
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,000
 And we can see here it's getting help from an integral protein.

149
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:06,000
 We'll span the plasma membrane and help with the passage of particular solids across the membrane.

150
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
 Then we have active transport.

151
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:14,000
 So active transport as its name implies requires energy.

152
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:22,000
 So the integral proteins will move molecules across the plasma membrane against their concentration gradient.

153
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:33,000
 So in order to go against their concentration gradient, meaning instead of going from high to low, it goes from areas of low concentration to high concentrations.

154
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,000
 And this requires energy to do so.

155
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:45,000
 So active transport, we can see that the integral proteins will move molecules across the plasma membrane with the aid of ATP.

156
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:55,000
 So use ATP as an energy source to actively pump substances across the plasma membrane against their concentration gradients.

157
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
 Okay.

158
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:11,000
 So again, just a quick recap of the different more types of membrane transport, facilitated fusion movement of molecules down their concentration gradient through the use of integral protein.

159
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:24,000
 Active transport requiring energy, requiring ATP in which the integral proteins will move molecules and pump them across the plasma membrane against their concentration gradient.

160
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:31,000
 Then we have more means of transport.

161
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
 Here we have endocytosis.

162
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:41,000
 Endo, meaning inner, so endocytosis is a mechanism by which particles enter cells.

163
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:42,000
 And we have different types.

164
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,000
 We have two main types here that we can see of endocytosis.

165
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:54,000
 It basically, depending upon the type of substance that is being moved.

166
00:16:54,000 --> 00:17:00,000
 So phagocytosis, and this is basically the cell engulfing solids or cell eating.

167
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,000
 Think of a Pac-Man cell.

168
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:13,000
 So if you've ever played Pac-Man, which is an old school video game, you can see Pac-Man kind of engulfing little pellets or, you know, those ghosts or whatever.

169
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,000
 So that would be an example of phagocytosis.

170
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:21,000
 Now, penocytosis is different from phagocytosis because it involves fluids.

171
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:29,000
 So penocytosis is cell drinking.

172
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:32,000
 We have receptor-mediated endocytosis.

173
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
 This is when plasma proteins will bind to certain molecules.

174
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:44,000
 It will then invaginate and form a coated pit and then pinch off to become a coated vesicle.

175
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:55,000
 This is a method by which hormones such as insulin or other hormones, enzymes, and low density lipoproteins can enter cells.

176
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:59,000
 So here we see the three types of endocytosis.

177
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:10,000
 We have phagocytosis when the cell will engulf a large particle and enclose it, sort of like a Pac-Man cell.

178
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:17,000
 We have penocytosis, which is basically the engulfing of fluids.

179
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:29,000
 So in folding of the plasma membrane will carry a drop of extra-cellular fluid containing solutes into the cell and forming a tiny membrane bound vesicle.

180
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:33,000
 No receptors are used, so process is non-specific.

181
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:39,000
 And then we have receptor-mediated endocytosis, so we have the use of receptors.

182
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:51,000
 So extra-cellular substances will bind to specific receptors or specific receptor proteins in regions of protein-coated pits.

183
00:18:51,000 --> 00:19:01,000
 This enables the cell to ingest and concentrate specific substances in protein-coated vesicles.

184
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:14,000
 This ingested substance can be simply released inside the cell or combined with a lysosome to ingest or digest whatever contents are within that vesicle.

185
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:22,000
 And then once this has occurred, receptors can be recycled and go back to the plasma membrane.

186
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:33,000
 So if endo means moving stuff into the cell exo, or exocytosis is movement of substances out of the cell.

187
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:38,000
 So exocytosis is a mechanism that moves substances out of the cell.

188
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:55,000
 A substance will usually be enclosed in a vesicle and then will move towards the plasma membrane where the contents of the vesicle will then erupt or move out of the vesicle into the extra-cellular fluid.

189
00:19:55,000 --> 00:20:09,000
 So here we can see the process of exocytosis. We have that vesicle, so the molecule to be secreted will migrate towards the plasma membrane in a membrane-bound vesicle.

190
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:22,000
 Once it reaches the plasma membrane, proteins will bind the vesicle to the plasma membrane and then the vesicle and plasma membrane will fuse.

191
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:31,000
 A pore will open up and the contents within the vesicle will be released to the exterior of the cell.

192
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:44,000
 Here we can see a really nice photo-micrograph of the contents of the vesicle being released via exocytosis to the outside of the cell.

193
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:58,000
 So the cytoplasm is a structure that lies internal or inside to the plasma membrane and consists of cytosol, organelles, and other inclusions.

194
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:07,000
 Cytosol is a jelly-like fluid in which other cellular elements are suspended and consists of water, ions, and enzymes.

195
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:20,000
 So the first organell or cytoplasmic organell that we're going to talk about are ribosomes. Ribosomes are constructed of proteins and ribosomal RNA and are not surrounded by a membrane.

196
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:26,000
 Ribosomes are important because this is the site of protein synthesis.

197
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:45,000
 We have an assembly of proteins called translation. Basically, the ribosomes are the assembly line of the manufacturing plants where substances are assembled to form proteins.

198
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:54,000
 Free ribosomes function within the cytosol and we have also ribosomes that are attached to the endoplasmic reticulum.

199
00:21:54,000 --> 00:22:05,000
 These ribosomes attached to the ER make proteins to help renew the plasma membrane, also make proteins that are exported from the cell.

200
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:16,000
 Speaking of the ER or the endoplasmic reticulum, the ER is a system of membrane wall envelopes and tubes throughout the cytoplasm and we have two different types of ER.

201
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:29,000
 We have rough ER which actually gets its appearance because we have ribosomes that are found within the external surfaces of the endoplasmic reticulum.

202
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:36,000
 So it kind of gives it that rough looking appearance and then we have smooth ER.

203
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:47,000
 So if it is smooth ER, there are no ribosomes attached. If there are no ribosomes, no protein synthesis occurs within the smooth ER.

204
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:55,000
 So the smooth ER consists of two-wheels in a branching network. Again, no ribosomes are attached to the smooth ER.

205
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:59,000
 That's why it has its smooth appearance.

206
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:11,000
 And here we can see the endoplasmic reticulum. We have the rough ER with the ribosomes kind of studied throughout the surface of the ER here.

207
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:17,000
 Again, this is where we have protein synthesis and then we have smooth ER.

208
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:23,000
 So no ribosomes, meaning no protein synthesis.

209
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:31,000
 The next organelle is the Golgi apparatus. The Golgi apparatus is a stack of three to ten disc-shaped envelopes.

210
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:38,000
 This organelle sorts products of the rough ER and then sends them to their proper destination.

211
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:49,000
 The products of the rough ER will move through the Golgi from the convex side to the concave side of the Golgi apparatus.

212
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:55,000
 So the Golgi apparatus is basically the packaging and shipping division of the manufacturing plant.

213
00:23:55,000 --> 00:24:01,000
 So it's the FedEx of the cell.

214
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:09,000
 So here we see many vesicles in the process of pinching off from the Golgi apparatus.

215
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:19,000
 So products coming from the rough ER being packaged and shipped through the Golgi apparatus.

216
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:34,000
 So this is the sequence of vents from the rough ER where protein synthesis takes place to the final distribution of these proteins once it has gone through packaging and shipping within the Golgi.

217
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:42,000
 So protein-contained vesicles will pinch off the rough ER, will migrate to fuse with membranes of the Golgi.

218
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:48,000
 Proteins are then modified within the Golgi compartments and are then packaged within different vesicle types.

219
00:24:48,000 --> 00:25:02,000
 So we can see they're being placed into these vesicles containing the proteins and then will then be shipped off depending on their ultimate destination.

220
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:12,000
 Sometimes it's towards the plasma membrane where the contents will then be shipped out of the cell.

221
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:26,000
 Or it could end up being incorporated into the plasma membrane or the proteins can then stay within the cell.

222
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:39,000
 And these proteins can be used as digestive enzymes to break down any particles that might come within the cell.

223
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:43,000
 The next organelle we're going to talk about is lysosomes.

224
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:48,000
 So lysosomes are membrane-walled sacs containing digestive enzymes.

225
00:25:48,000 --> 00:26:01,000
 These digest any unwanted substances and are also known as the demolition crew, so basically they break down and digest any unwanted substances within the cell.

226
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:08,000
 Here's just some pictures of an electron micrograph of lysosomes containing those enzymes.

227
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:14,000
 And then we can see the lightening areas are regions where materials are being digested.

228
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:19,000
 A very important organelle that we talked about earlier is the mitochondria.

229
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:26,000
 So the mitochondria are surrounded by a double-walled membrane and generate most of the cell's energy.

230
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:30,000
 They are known as the power plant or the powerhouse of the cell.

231
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:43,000
 The mitochondria release energy stored in chemical bonds and will transfer this energy to help produce ATP, also known as adenotryphosphate, basically what we use for energy.

232
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:51,000
 So cells with very high energy requirements will have more mitochondria like we talked about earlier.

233
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:57,000
 For example, muscle cells will need more mitochondria because they need more ATP to function.

234
00:26:57,000 --> 00:27:00,000
 The mitochondria are the most complex organelle.

235
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:09,000
 They actually contain some eternally inherited DNA, so we get our mitochondria from our mothers.

236
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:14,000
 We also think that the mitochondria might have arisen from bacteria.

237
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:20,000
 So here we can see the structure of the mitochondrial DNA.

238
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:27,000
 We have an outer membrane and then an inner membrane, and then we see these folds known as Christe.

239
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:36,000
 And then we have enzymes within the Christe of the mitochondria.

240
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:39,000
 Next, organelle are peroxisomes.

241
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:44,000
 These are membrane-walled sacks of oxidase enzymes.

242
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:51,000
 Peroxisomes are enzymes that help neutralize any free radicals and help break down poisons.

243
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:54,000
 They also can break down long chains of fatty acids.

244
00:27:54,000 --> 00:28:00,000
 Peroxisomes are very numerous in the liver and kidneys because they basically help detoxify things.

245
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:05,000
 We know that liver and kidneys are very important in detoxification of our blood.

246
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:14,000
 Peroxisomes are the toxic waste removal system of this cell.

247
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
 Then we have the cytoskeleton.

248
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:20,000
 This is the cell skeleton, which is an elaborate network of rods.

249
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:23,000
 And we have three different types of rods.

250
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:27,000
 We have microtubules, microfilaments, and intermediate filaments.

251
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:34,000
 So microtubules being cylindrical structures made up of proteins, microfilaments being filaments

252
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:43,000
 of contractile protein actin, very important when we talk about movement and contraction

253
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:48,000
 of these proteins, specifically in skeletal muscle.

254
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:55,000
 And then we have intermediate filaments, which are protein fibers.

255
00:28:55,000 --> 00:29:00,000
 So microfilaments are strands made of spherical protein subunit called actins.

256
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:11,000
 And you can see that here, kind of crisscrossing and being closely associated with one another.

257
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:15,000
 Microfilaments form the blue networks surrounding the pink nucleus in the photo.

258
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:18,000
 So again, these are part of the cytoskeleton.

259
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:21,000
 We then have intermediate filaments.

260
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:28,000
 These are tough in soluble protein fibers that are constructed like woven ropes.

261
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
 And then we have microtubules.

262
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:35,000
 These are hollow tubes of spherical protein subunits called tubulins.

263
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:42,000
 Again, helping form that cytoskeletal element within the cell.

264
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:46,000
 Other organelles include centrosomes and centrioles.

265
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:56,000
 Centrosome is a spherical structure in the cytoplasm composed of the centrosome matrix and centrioles.

266
00:29:56,000 --> 00:30:02,000
 So centrosomes being made up of centrioles.

267
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:11,000
 Centrioles are paired cylindrical bodies, consists of 27 short microtubules, also act in forming cilia,

268
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:18,000
 and are very necessary for an event called karyokinesis or nuclear division.

269
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:23,000
 So this occurs during mitosis.

270
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:33,000
 We also have cytoplasmic inclusions which are temporary structures and not present in all cell types.

271
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:39,000
 These inclusions may consist of pigments, crystals of protein as well as food stores.

272
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:43,000
 So such inclusions include lipid droplets.

273
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:47,000
 These are found in liver cells and fat cells.

274
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:53,000
 We also have glycosomes which help store sugar in the form of glycogen.

275
00:30:53,000 --> 00:31:02,000
 So we know that glucose is very important to help us perform certain functions.

276
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:10,000
 And then the store form of glucose is known as glycogen.

277
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:17,000
 So the next very important organelle is the nucleus, nucleus is the control center of the cell.

278
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:23,000
 We know that DNA directs the cell's activities, DNA is contained within the nucleus.

279
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:27,000
 DNA is important for providing instructions for protein synthesis.

280
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:30,000
 You know, we get our DNA from our parents.

281
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:38,000
 And protein synthesis is important because it basically directs things such as eye color or hair color

282
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:44,000
 or, you know, all sorts of things, sorts of traits that we get from our parents.

283
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:50,000
 So the nucleus is approximately five microns in diameter.

284
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:53,000
 And here we see the purple structure.

285
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:57,000
 It's not usually purple, but in this figure it is purple.

286
00:31:57,000 --> 00:32:03,000
 We can see the nucleus as well as the nucleolus.

287
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:08,000
 And we see that it is surrounded by cisterns of the rough ER.

288
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:14,000
 Again, rough ER containing ribosomes which is very important in protein synthesis.

289
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:22,000
 And we know that the DNA within the nucleus directs protein synthesis.

290
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:25,000
 So the nucleus is surrounded by the nuclear envelope.

291
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:29,000
 These are two parallel membranes separated by a fluid filled space.

292
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:38,000
 We have nuclear pores that penetrate the nuclear envelope which allow large molecules to pass in and out of the nucleus.

293
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:41,000
 Then we have within the nucleus the nucleolus.

294
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:48,000
 This is also known as the little nucleus in the center of the nucleus contains parts of several chromosomes

295
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:54,000
 and is the site of ribosomes subunit assembly.

296
00:32:54,000 --> 00:33:00,000
 So speaking of chromatin and chromosomes we have what is known as DNA.

297
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:05,000
 DNA is your deoxyribonucleic acid.

298
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:10,000
 Basically DNA is a double helix composed of four subunits.

299
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:17,000
 We have our thymine, adenine, cytosine and guanine.

300
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:19,000
 We have purines and pyrimidines.

301
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:23,000
 A way to remember that is pure as gold.

302
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:36,000
 So purines include the subunits of adenine and guanine and the pyrimidines are thymine and cytosine.

303
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:39,000
 So DNA is packed with protein molecules.

304
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:46,000
 DNA is plus proteins form chromatin.

305
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:54,000
 So each cluster of DNA and histone proteins is considered to be called a nucleosome.

306
00:33:54,000 --> 00:34:04,000
 Here we see the double helix structure of DNA and we have pairings of different nucleotides or different subunits

307
00:34:04,000 --> 00:34:14,000
 so we can see that adenine will bind with thymine and this bond is through a hydrogen bond.

308
00:34:14,000 --> 00:34:21,000
 And then we see the pairing of cytosine with guanine.

309
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:36,000
 So purines and pyrimidines will basically bind with one another and form this double helical structure that we know to be DNA.

310
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:49,000
 So we have different regions within the DNA. Extended chromatin is the active region where the DNA genetic code will be copied into messenger RNA in transcription.

311
00:34:49,000 --> 00:35:00,000
 Basically messenger RNA takes that code from the DNA to allow for protein synthesis.

312
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:12,000
 We also have condensed chromatin. These are tightly coiled nucleosomes. This is the inactive form of chromatin.

313
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:31,000
 So chromosomes are the highest level of organization of chromatin. Chromosomes contain a long molecule of DNA and a typical human cell contains 46 chromosomes or 23 pairs of chromosomes.

314
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:43,000
 One for mom, one from dad. So here we see the DNA double helix. We have the active region known as the extended chromatin.

315
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:53,000
 And then we can see how they start to tightly bind it condensed to then form the highest level which is the chromosome.

316
00:35:53,000 --> 00:36:05,000
 Now that we've learned what makes up our cells and the different structures that make up our cells, we have to ask ourselves what do our cells do?

317
00:36:05,000 --> 00:36:11,000
 Do all cells have the same structure and function? To answer that question, let's compare these two cells.

318
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:22,000
 On the left we have microglia cells in the brain. Microglia cells are support cells for the main cells in the brain, the neurons.

319
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:33,000
 So we can see the microglia cells shaded green here. And then on the right we see a sperm cell. Now what do we notice about their structures?

320
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:44,000
 We know they have the body of the cell, there's organelles within the cell. We tell that there are two specific structures that differentiate these two cells.

321
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:54,000
 We know that the sperm cell is responsible for uniting with an egg cell and forming a totally new organism.

322
00:36:54,000 --> 00:36:59,000
 But let's take a look at what microglia cells do in the brain.

323
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:09,000
 So pay attention to how their structure relates to function. So here are microglia cells in a living mouse brain.

324
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:24,000
 We can see the green stained microglia cells. Now when the mouse brain has some sort of injury, injury to the neurons,

325
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:29,000
 the microglia cells are important because they help they actually increase.

326
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:35,000
 So we can see there's a larger number of microglia cells going to the site of injury within the brain.

327
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:44,000
 They act like pac-man cells or macrophages to take away any cellular debris in any damaged cells.

328
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:54,000
 So if we damage the neurons within the mouse brain, these support cells, these microglia cells, will come in and take away this damage.

329
00:37:54,000 --> 00:38:09,000
 So again, we can see that the structure of the cell does relate to its function. The sperm cell that we saw earlier has to swim into the vaginal canal,

330
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:16,000
 swim and meet the female's egg in order for fertilization in order for new life to occur.

331
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:30,000
 So we saw that the structure of the sperm was ideal. It had a tail, the head of the sperm, important for getting into the, through the layers of the egg cell for fertilization.

332
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:44,000
 Whereas in the mouse brain, these microglia have specific cell structure that allows them to kind of phagocytize or eat any damaged cells or cellular debris.

333
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:50,000
 So again, pay attention to how structures of cell relates to their function.

334
00:38:50,000 --> 00:39:02,000
 Okay, so that's it for this part of chapter three. There will be another video focusing on membrane transport, and we'll get more into that in the next video.

